1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,700
For as long as there have been
people, we have watched birds
and

2
00:00:03,710 --> 00:00:05,330
wondered what it would be like
to fly.

3
00:00:06,083 --> 00:00:08,383
Ancient myths are full of flying
people.

4
00:00:08,963 --> 00:00:12,343
Icarus, in Greek legend, made
wings of feathers and wax,

5
00:00:12,823 --> 00:00:14,663
and fell when the sun melted
them.

6
00:00:15,333 --> 00:00:18,663
Around fifteen hundred, Leonardo
da Vinci filled

7
00:00:18,673 --> 00:00:21,863
notebooks with designs for
flying machines with

8
00:00:21,873 --> 00:00:22,713
flapping wings.

9
00:00:23,453 --> 00:00:24,913
None of them could ever have
flown.

10
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:28,967
Then, on a cold, windy morning
in December nineteen

11
00:00:28,977 --> 00:00:32,360
oh three, two brothers who ran a
bicycle shop did

12
00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:33,170
it for real.

13
00:00:33,667 --> 00:00:35,497
Their flight lasted twelve
seconds.

14
00:00:35,507 --> 00:00:38,997
Sixty six years later, humans
walked on

15
00:00:39,007 --> 00:00:39,807
the Moon.

16
00:00:40,167 --> 00:00:42,127
This is how humans learned to
fly.

17
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:47,220
The first humans to leave the
ground did it in a bag of hot
air.

18
00:00:47,917 --> 00:00:51,067
In Paris, in November seventeen
eighty three,

19
00:00:51,077 --> 00:00:54,227
a balloon built by the
Montgolfier brothers carried

20
00:00:54,237 --> 00:00:57,347
two men over the rooftops for
about twenty five

21
00:00:57,357 --> 00:00:58,157
minutes.

22
00:00:58,833 --> 00:01:01,513
Two months earlier, at the
palace of Versailles,

23
00:01:01,793 --> 00:01:05,263
the Montgolfiers had tested
their balloon with the first air

24
00:01:05,273 --> 00:01:08,333
passengers in history: a sheep,
a duck

25
00:01:08,733 --> 00:01:09,533
and a rooster.

26
00:01:09,793 --> 00:01:11,133
All three landed safely.

27
00:01:12,083 --> 00:01:14,423
But balloons drift wherever the
wind takes them.

28
00:01:14,823 --> 00:01:16,883
To truly fly, you need a wing.

29
00:01:17,667 --> 00:01:21,077
In seventeen ninety nine, an
English baronet named

30
00:01:21,087 --> 00:01:24,097
George Cayley sketched the basic
layout of a modern

31
00:01:24,107 --> 00:01:24,907
aeroplane:

32
00:01:25,187 --> 00:01:27,927
a fixed wing for lift, a tail
for balance,

33
00:01:28,347 --> 00:01:30,307
and something separate to push
it forward.

34
00:01:31,083 --> 00:01:34,273
Fifty years later, one of his
gliders carried a

35
00:01:34,283 --> 00:01:37,323
frightened passenger across a
valley in Yorkshire.

36
00:01:37,783 --> 00:01:39,843
By some accounts, it was his
coachman.

37
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:43,630
In Germany in the eighteen
nineties, an engineer named Otto

38
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:47,030
Lilienthal made around two
thousand flights in gliders he

39
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:50,420
built himself, running down a
hill and launching into the
wind.

40
00:01:51,167 --> 00:01:54,397
Newspapers around the world
printed photographs of the

41
00:01:54,407 --> 00:01:55,207
flying man.

42
00:01:55,833 --> 00:01:58,923
In eighteen ninety six, a gust
stalled his glider,

43
00:01:58,933 --> 00:01:59,733
and he fell.

44
00:02:00,153 --> 00:02:01,193
He died the next day.

45
00:02:01,917 --> 00:02:05,207
Far away, in Dayton, Ohio, two
brothers read the

46
00:02:05,217 --> 00:02:05,487
news,

47
00:02:05,497 --> 00:02:08,197
and decided to take up where he
left off.

48
00:02:09,817 --> 00:02:13,107
Neither Wilbur nor Orville
Wright ever received a high
school

49
00:02:13,117 --> 00:02:13,917
diploma.

50
00:02:14,197 --> 00:02:17,387
They built printing presses,
then opened a shop selling and

51
00:02:17,397 --> 00:02:18,197
repairing bicycles.

52
00:02:19,333 --> 00:02:21,293
Bicycles taught them something
important:

53
00:02:21,953 --> 00:02:23,333
a machine can be unstable,

54
00:02:24,213 --> 00:02:26,533
as long as the rider can control
it.

55
00:02:27,167 --> 00:02:30,577
Their sister Katharine, a
teacher, helped run the family

56
00:02:30,587 --> 00:02:32,917
home and the business while they
experimented,

57
00:02:32,927 --> 00:02:35,507
and became one of their
strongest supporters.

58
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:39,910
Everyone else was trying to
build aircraft that were stable
on

59
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:40,720
their own.

60
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:42,980
The Wrights thought the real
problem was control.

61
00:02:43,833 --> 00:02:47,303
Wilbur watched buzzards twisting
the tips of their wings to turn

62
00:02:47,313 --> 00:02:48,113
and balance.

63
00:02:48,917 --> 00:02:52,267
Then, idly twisting a long,
empty cardboard box

64
00:02:52,277 --> 00:02:55,747
in the shop, he realised a whole
wing could be twisted

65
00:02:55,757 --> 00:02:56,877
in the same way.

66
00:02:57,317 --> 00:02:58,777
They called it wing warping.

67
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:01,200
For testing,

68
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:04,600
they needed steady winds and
soft sand to land on.

69
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:08,390
The weather bureau suggested a
remote beach called Kitty

70
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:08,770
Hawk,

71
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:10,220
in North Carolina.

72
00:03:11,083 --> 00:03:11,883
Strictly speaking,

73
00:03:12,603 --> 00:03:15,553
they flew among the sand dunes
of Kill Devil Hills,

74
00:03:15,563 --> 00:03:18,563
about four miles south of the
village.

75
00:03:19,183 --> 00:03:22,103
There was no road, and supplies
came by boat.

76
00:03:22,833 --> 00:03:26,263
Their gliders of nineteen
hundred and nineteen oh one

77
00:03:26,273 --> 00:03:26,803
flew,

78
00:03:26,813 --> 00:03:29,283
but they produced far less lift
than the published tables

79
00:03:29,293 --> 00:03:30,093
predicted.

80
00:03:30,750 --> 00:03:34,240
Wilbur was so discouraged he
said that men would not fly

81
00:03:34,250 --> 00:03:35,430
in a thousand years.

82
00:03:37,067 --> 00:03:39,427
Back home, they built their own
wind tunnel,

83
00:03:39,987 --> 00:03:42,147
a wooden box with a fan at one
end.

84
00:03:42,833 --> 00:03:45,963
Inside, they tested around two
hundred small

85
00:03:45,973 --> 00:03:49,473
model wings, carefully measuring
the lift each one made.

86
00:03:50,167 --> 00:03:53,437
They discovered that the numbers
everyone had relied on were

87
00:03:53,447 --> 00:03:54,247
wrong.

88
00:03:54,267 --> 00:03:57,007
Now they had their own data, the
best in the world.

89
00:03:57,750 --> 00:04:00,570
A wing works by meeting the air
at a slight angle.

90
00:04:01,030 --> 00:04:04,450
Air flowing over the curved top
is pulled downward behind it,

91
00:04:04,810 --> 00:04:05,930
and the wing is pushed up.

92
00:04:06,230 --> 00:04:07,250
That push is lift.

93
00:04:08,083 --> 00:04:11,273
In nineteen oh two, their new
glider added a movable

94
00:04:11,283 --> 00:04:12,083
rudder.

95
00:04:12,223 --> 00:04:14,603
Now they could control it on all
three axes:

96
00:04:15,303 --> 00:04:16,803
pitch, roll and yaw.

97
00:04:17,417 --> 00:04:18,957
They made hundreds of glides.

98
00:04:19,577 --> 00:04:22,767
That system of three-axis
control is still how

99
00:04:22,777 --> 00:04:24,637
every aeroplane is flown today.

100
00:04:25,333 --> 00:04:27,533
They spent months at Kitty Hawk
each year,

101
00:04:27,933 --> 00:04:31,383
living in a wooden shed,
battling storms and swarms of

102
00:04:31,393 --> 00:04:32,193
mosquitoes.

103
00:04:33,733 --> 00:04:35,233
Next, they needed an engine.

104
00:04:35,933 --> 00:04:37,913
No company could build one light
enough,

105
00:04:38,413 --> 00:04:39,213
so their mechanic,

106
00:04:39,593 --> 00:04:42,523
Charlie Taylor, built one in
about six weeks,

107
00:04:42,533 --> 00:04:44,393
with an aluminium block.

108
00:04:45,167 --> 00:04:48,247
And they realised a propeller is
just a wing that spins.

109
00:04:48,947 --> 00:04:51,787
Nobody had worked out how to
design one properly,

110
00:04:52,287 --> 00:04:53,227
so they did it themselves.

111
00:04:54,250 --> 00:04:57,470
The finished Flyer had wings
more than twelve metres across.

112
00:04:58,250 --> 00:05:01,080
The pilot lay flat on his
stomach on the lower wing,

113
00:05:01,090 --> 00:05:03,890
and steered by shifting his hips
in a cradle,

114
00:05:04,330 --> 00:05:06,350
which pulled the wires that
warped the wings.

115
00:05:07,333 --> 00:05:08,133
Meanwhile,

116
00:05:08,413 --> 00:05:11,903
the famous scientist Samuel
Langley had fifty thousand
dollars

117
00:05:11,913 --> 00:05:14,963
from the United States Army to
build a flying

118
00:05:14,973 --> 00:05:15,773
machine.

119
00:05:16,093 --> 00:05:19,103
On the eighth of December,
nineteen oh three,

120
00:05:19,113 --> 00:05:21,473
it plunged straight into the
Potomac River.

121
00:05:22,250 --> 00:05:25,420
The Wright brothers spent about
a thousand dollars of their own

122
00:05:25,430 --> 00:05:26,230
money.

123
00:05:27,317 --> 00:05:30,487
On the fourteenth of December,
nineteen oh three,

124
00:05:30,497 --> 00:05:33,577
the brothers tossed a coin to
decide who would go first.

125
00:05:34,257 --> 00:05:37,017
Wilbur won, but he stalled the
machine on takeoff.

126
00:05:37,917 --> 00:05:40,857
Three days later, on the
seventeenth of December,

127
00:05:41,257 --> 00:05:42,337
it was Orville's turn.

128
00:05:42,897 --> 00:05:46,057
The wind was blowing at more
than twenty miles an hour.

129
00:05:46,750 --> 00:05:50,140
At ten thirty five in the
morning, the Flyer ran along a
wooden

130
00:05:50,150 --> 00:05:50,950
rail,

131
00:05:50,970 --> 00:05:52,990
lifted into the air, and stayed
there.

132
00:05:53,667 --> 00:05:57,037
It flew for twelve seconds and
covered thirty seven

133
00:05:57,047 --> 00:05:57,597
metres,

134
00:05:57,607 --> 00:06:01,047
a shorter distance than the
wingspan of a modern jumbo jet.

135
00:06:01,750 --> 00:06:03,010
Wilbur ran alongside.

136
00:06:03,830 --> 00:06:07,300
A local lifeguard named John
Daniels, who had never

137
00:06:07,310 --> 00:06:10,660
used a camera before, took one
of the most famous

138
00:06:10,670 --> 00:06:12,270
photographs ever made.

139
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:14,980
They flew three more times that
day.

140
00:06:15,460 --> 00:06:18,330
The last flight, by Wilbur,
lasted fifty nine

141
00:06:18,340 --> 00:06:21,617
seconds and covered over two
hundred and fifty

142
00:06:21,627 --> 00:06:22,427
metres.

143
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:24,860
Orville sent a telegram to their
father:

144
00:06:25,240 --> 00:06:26,040
Success.

145
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:27,930
Four flights Thursday morning.

146
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:29,280
Inform press.

147
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:30,920
Home Christmas.

148
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:34,080
Almost nobody believed them.

149
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:37,530
Newspapers ignored the story,
and many experts

150
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:38,980
called them liars.

151
00:06:39,750 --> 00:06:43,240
Back in Ohio, they kept
improving their machine in a cow

152
00:06:43,250 --> 00:06:44,950
pasture called Huffman Prairie.

153
00:06:45,590 --> 00:06:48,600
By nineteen oh five, they could
stay up for thirty nine

154
00:06:48,610 --> 00:06:49,410
minutes,

155
00:06:49,710 --> 00:06:50,890
circling over the fields.

156
00:06:51,750 --> 00:06:54,960
Then, in nineteen oh eight,
Wilbur flew in

157
00:06:54,970 --> 00:06:58,030
public at a racecourse near Le
Mans, in France.

158
00:06:58,710 --> 00:07:02,070
He banked, circled and landed
exactly where he chose.

159
00:07:02,833 --> 00:07:03,893
The crowds were stunned.

160
00:07:04,393 --> 00:07:07,173
One French aviator said: we are
beaten.

161
00:07:07,733 --> 00:07:08,533
We do not exist.

162
00:07:09,583 --> 00:07:11,183
Flying was still dangerous.

163
00:07:11,923 --> 00:07:14,973
That September, Orville crashed
during a demonstration

164
00:07:14,983 --> 00:07:16,463
for the US Army.

165
00:07:16,983 --> 00:07:19,683
His passenger, Lieutenant Thomas
Selfridge,

166
00:07:20,043 --> 00:07:23,033
became the first person to die
in a powered aeroplane

167
00:07:23,043 --> 00:07:23,843
crash.

168
00:07:24,667 --> 00:07:27,467
But the Army bought a Wright
Flyer the following year,

169
00:07:27,927 --> 00:07:30,207
and the brothers became famous
around the world.

170
00:07:31,817 --> 00:07:33,817
After that, aviation exploded.

171
00:07:34,377 --> 00:07:37,767
In nineteen oh nine, Louis
Blériot flew across the English

172
00:07:37,777 --> 00:07:38,577
Channel.

173
00:07:38,917 --> 00:07:42,317
In nineteen twenty seven,
Charles Lindbergh flew alone,

174
00:07:42,997 --> 00:07:45,017
nonstop, from New York to Paris.

175
00:07:45,917 --> 00:07:48,867
Twenty years after that, Chuck
Yeager flew

176
00:07:48,877 --> 00:07:50,737
faster than the speed of sound.

177
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:54,570
In the late nineteen thirties,
Frank Whittle in Britain

178
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:57,990
and Hans von Ohain in Germany
each invented the jet

179
00:07:58,000 --> 00:07:58,800
engine.

180
00:07:58,980 --> 00:08:02,122
The first jet aircraft flew in
August nineteen thirty

181
00:08:02,132 --> 00:08:02,932
nine.

182
00:08:03,167 --> 00:08:06,137
And in July nineteen sixty nine,
Neil Armstrong

183
00:08:06,147 --> 00:08:07,427
landed on the Moon.

184
00:08:07,987 --> 00:08:11,437
In his kit, he carried a piece
of fabric from the wing of

185
00:08:11,447 --> 00:08:13,547
the nineteen oh three Wright
Flyer.

186
00:08:14,083 --> 00:08:17,513
In nineteen fifty two, the de
Havilland Comet began the

187
00:08:17,523 --> 00:08:19,163
first jet airliner service.

188
00:08:19,743 --> 00:08:22,333
And in nineteen seventy, the
Boeing seven four seven

189
00:08:22,343 --> 00:08:25,793
made long distance flying
affordable for

190
00:08:25,803 --> 00:08:26,603
millions.

191
00:08:27,083 --> 00:08:30,433
Today, around a hundred thousand
commercial flights take

192
00:08:30,443 --> 00:08:31,383
off every day.

193
00:08:32,083 --> 00:08:35,293
The original nineteen oh three
Flyer spent twenty years

194
00:08:35,303 --> 00:08:38,613
on display in London, after a
dispute with the

195
00:08:38,623 --> 00:08:39,423
Smithsonian.

196
00:08:39,843 --> 00:08:42,833
Since nineteen forty eight, it
has hung in Washington,

197
00:08:42,843 --> 00:08:45,933
D.C., in what is now the
National Air and Space

198
00:08:45,943 --> 00:08:46,743
Museum.

199
00:08:47,333 --> 00:08:49,953
Wilbur never saw much of the age
he started.

200
00:08:50,533 --> 00:08:53,437
He died of typhoid fever in
nineteen twelve,

201
00:08:53,447 --> 00:08:55,503
aged just forty five.

202
00:08:55,513 --> 00:08:57,423
Orville lived until nineteen
forty eight,

203
00:08:57,433 --> 00:09:00,583
long enough to see an aircraft
fly faster than

204
00:09:00,593 --> 00:09:01,393
sound.

205
00:09:02,567 --> 00:09:05,597
It all began with two brothers
from a bicycle shop,

206
00:09:05,607 --> 00:09:08,897
who refused to give up, and
treated flying not

207
00:09:08,907 --> 00:09:11,587
as a dream, but as an
engineering problem.

208
00:09:12,333 --> 00:09:14,313
Twelve seconds over a beach.

209
00:09:14,813 --> 00:09:16,373
And the whole sky opened up.
